<section class="page-head">
  <p class="kicker">Part II &mdash; The Inside of Things &middot; Chapters 2 and 3</p>
  <h1>The Infinitesimal Self</h1>
  <p class="lede muted">A universe of flawlessly described matter could still be utterly dark within. The
    second movement asks where experience enters &mdash; and answers with one symbol from calculus.</p>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame frame--first illuminated">
    <p class="kicker">2.1</p>
    <h2 id="t-dx">dx, and the Integral of a Life</h2>
    <p class="muted small">Taken further on its own page: <a href="/dx/">dx as the smallest possible
      integral</a>, and the price an emerging awareness pays out of memory.</p>
    <p class="lede">A single moment of experience, taken entirely on its own, is vanishingly small: no
      duration, no meaning, a dx flickering at the edge of the first infinity.</p>
    <p>Consciousness, on this reading, is <strong>the integral sign</strong> &mdash; the operation that
      gathers an uncountable infinity of meaningless instants into one finite, bounded, located experience
      of being someone. The self is not the contents of awareness. It is the gathering itself: the running
      sum.</p>
    <p>The reframing is worth stating precisely, because it is the chapter's whole claim. The hard problem
      asks how matter produces awareness. This asks instead how <em>awareness is the act of integration
      that matter performs</em>. Whether that is an answer or a relocation of the question is exactly the
      argument to have. <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></p>
    <p class="muted small">Grading: <span class="sci">&#9670; established</span> &middot;
      <span class="hist">&#9670; scholarship</span> &middot;
      <span class="spec">&#9670; the framework's argument, contested</span> &middot;
      <span class="myth">&#9670; vision, stated as vision</span>.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<div class="divider">✦</div>

<section class="wrap">
  <div class="page-head tight">
    <p class="kicker">2.2 and 2.3</p>
    <h2 id="t-two-systems">Two Systems, One Operation</h2>
    <p class="lede muted">The framework points at two places in the body where an integral is not a
      metaphor but a mechanism.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="grid two">
    <div class="frame card">
      <h3>The inner ear <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></h3>
      <p>The semicircular canals do not measure where you are. They measure angular acceleration, and the
        nervous system integrates that signal &mdash; twice &mdash; to recover velocity and position. Your
        sense of standing upright is a running sum over infinitesimal increments you never consciously
        meet. This part is ordinary, well-measured physiology.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="frame card">
      <h3>The microtubule lattice <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></h3>
      <p>The framework sites the gathering in the helical microtubule lattices threaded through neurons,
        and in the Penrose&ndash;Hameroff proposal that each minute quantum collapse within them is a
        single grain of experience. This is a live and contested research programme, not a settled one,
        and the site treats it that way.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

<aside class="fundamental" aria-labelledby="t-fundamental-two">
    <p class="fundamental-tag">Fundamental&nbsp;II</p>
    <div class="fundamental-head">
      <h3 id="t-fundamental-two">A self is an operation, not a thing.</h3>
      <p>The second fundamental, and the one readers resist hardest. Nothing in this work treats a self as a substance that persists and occasionally changes. A self is an <em>integral</em>: a running sum, taken continuously, over moments too small to be experienced individually. <strong>If you keep picturing a thing rather than an operation, the chapters on the splice, the seam and the ledger will all read as mysticism</strong> &mdash; because each of them is about what happens to a sum, not about what happens to an object.</p>
    </div>

    <figure id="fig-integral" class="plate">
    <figcaption class="plate-cap"><span class="plate-num">Plate II</span> The Self as the Running Sum
      <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></figcaption>
