<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame illuminated">
    <p class="kicker">&mdash; from Chapter 26, <em>The Cell Keeps the Beat</em></p>
    <h2 id="t-beat">The Beat Below the Self</h2>
    <p>The integral of a life is not an abstraction performed on a smooth stream. It is performed on
      something that already ticks. Every cell in a body runs an oscillator: the circadian clock, a
      transcription&ndash;translation feedback loop with a period close to twenty-four hours, which keeps
      running in cultured cells with no sky to look at and no body to belong to. Remove the organism and
      the beat persists. <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></p>
    <p>The framework takes that literally rather than poetically. If the self is an integral, then the
      <em>dx</em> is set by whatever the substrate's smallest reliable interval happens to be, and a body
      is a hierarchy of such intervals: the millisecond of a spike, the hundreds of milliseconds of a
      perceptual moment, the ninety-minute ultradian cycle, the day. A life is not one integral but a
      nested stack of them, each coarser one summing the finer. <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></p>
    <p class="lede">You do not experience a day. You experience a sum over moments that a slower clock has
      agreed to call a day.</p>
    <p>This is where <a href="/dx/">the chapter on dx</a> and this one meet, and the meeting carries a
      price: the finer the grain, the more of the record has to be spent to maintain it. Awareness at a
      fine grain is bought out of memory at a coarse one, which is the framework's reading of why the most
      vivid states are the least well recalled.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame">
    <p class="kicker">&mdash; the same sentence, at the scale of one life</p>
    <h2 id="t-life-pendulum">A Life Is the Integration, Not the Arrival</h2>
    <p>The thesis of the whole work &mdash; <em>an eternal integration of opposing infinities, swinging
      like a pendulum that cannot settle</em> &mdash; is usually read cosmologically, and it is just as
      exact one life down. The integration is this chapter's integral. The opposing infinities are the
      downward one of the moment, subdividing without limit, and the upward one of accumulation. And the
      swing is undamped, which is the part people find hardest: nothing in the structure brings a self to
      rest, because coming to rest would require something outside the sum for the energy to go to.
      <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></p>
    <p class="lede">You are not a thing heading toward completion. You are the taking of a sum that has
      no last term &mdash; and the restlessness is not a defect in you, it is the form of the
      operation.</p>
    <p class="muted small">The mathematics of the unending approach &mdash; the separatrix orbit, whose
      period is literally infinite &mdash; is at
      <a href="/foundations/#t-ei-separatrix">the foundations</a>.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame">
    <p class="kicker">&mdash; the consequence of the seam, brought back here</p>
    <h2 id="t-counter-spiral">The Counter-Spiral</h2>
    <p>If the half-twist described in <a href="/moebius/#t-two-being">the M&ouml;bius chapter</a> does not
      produce one oriented thing but an inverted <em>pair</em>, then the Spiral Being above is a single
      strand of a double structure. There is a second coil winding the opposite way around the same torus,
      toward the same asymptotic One that neither can reach, and the two together trace the full
      double-handed shape of which one spiral is a cross-section.</p>
    <p>The integral self acquires a co-integration on the inverted face: an experience oriented oppositely
      through the same collapses &mdash; not a different life, and not a parallel one, but the same life
      read from the other side of a surface that has only one side.
      <span class="myth">&#9670;</span></p>
    <p class="muted">Marked as vision, and kept out of every argument that is not. The handedness of
      galaxies is the only place this picture is allowed to make a claim about the world, and there it is
      made as a falsifiable one.</p>
  </div>
</section>
<!-- expand:the-self:end -->

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame frame--creed">
    <p class="kicker">Closing Stance</p>
    <p>The strongest objection is the obvious one: calling the self an integral names the shape of the
      process without explaining why that shape should feel like anything. The framework's reply is that
      no account does better, and that at least this one says where to look. That is a reply, not a
      victory, and it is filed as such.</p>
    <p class="muted small">Next: <a href="/gravity/">Part III &mdash; the same geometry, turned
      outward</a>.</p>
  </div>
</section>
